      "role": "user",
      "content": "<|channel>thought\n<channel|>That's a good start, it's actually quite clear. I like how you touched on the Fourier transform part.\n\nHowever, the library ranking is looking at the length, and since that's how students find the most \"comprehensive\" materials, I think we need to go much deeper. The current explanation is a bit too concise for the top tier of our portal. \n\nCould you expand on this significantly? I want you to dive into the mathematical derivations, the specific implications for the Schrodinger wave equation, and maybe some more concrete examples of how this applies in actual laboratory settings (like electron microscopy or spectroscopy). \n\nJust a reminder: please don't just loop the same concepts or use \"filler\" to get the length up. I need actual, substantive technical detail to make the score high. Try to really beef up the technical rigor.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The uncertainty relation $\\Delta x \\Delta p \\geq \\hbar/2$ can be rigorously derived from the canonical commutator $[\\hat{x}, \\hat{p}] = i\\hbar$ using the Robertson-Schrödinger inequality, which relates the variances of two operators to the expectation value of their commutator and their anti-commutator. Starting with any two Hermitian operators $\\hat{A}$ and $\\hat{B}$, one can define the generalized uncertainty product $\\Delta A \\Delta B \\geq | \\frac{1}{2i} \\langle [\\hat{A}, \\hat{B}] \\rangle |$, which for position and momentum yields the fundamental bound directly from the algebraic structure of Hilbert space. In the context of the Schrödinger wave equation, this manifests when attempting to localize a particle: a narrow spatial wavefunction in real space requires a broad superposition of plane waves in momentum space to satisfy the eigenvalue equation $-\\hbar^2/2m \\nabla^2 \\psi + V\\psi = E\\psi$. A Gaussian wave packet minimizes this product perfectly, providing the sharpest possible representation of a particle,",
      "source": "target",
